const TEXTS_EN={
en_a1_t1:'Hi, I’m Tom. I live in a small flat in Manchester with my wife, Sara, and our two children. The flat has two bedrooms, a kitchen and a living room. My parents live in a big house near the sea. We visit them every Sunday.',
 en_a1_t2:'My name is Lucy and I live in Bristol, in the city centre. I work in a chemist’s near my house, so I walk to work. On Saturdays, I go to the beach by bus with my friends. In the evening, we have dinner in a small restaurant.',
 en_a2_t1:'Last August, Marta travelled to Ireland with her best friend, Claire. They flew from Madrid to Dublin and then took a bus to Galway, on the west coast. They stayed in a small hotel near the station. The weather was cooler than in Spain, and it rained almost every day, but they loved the music in the pubs. Next year, they are going to visit Scotland by train.',
 en_a2_t2:'Last week, Daniel felt terrible. On Monday he had a sore throat, and on Tuesday he had a high temperature, so he stayed in bed. On Wednesday he went to the doctor. She told him to drink lots of water and rest for three days. Now he is feeling much better, and he is going to start running again next week.',
 en_b1_t1:'Have you ever read a book after seeing the film? Last month I watched an old thriller called The Silent Island, and I enjoyed it so much that I decided to read the novel it was based on. The book was written in 1962 by a Scottish journalist who had never published anything before. To my surprise, the story is quite different: in the novel, the main character is a woman, and the ending is much darker. My friend told me that the producers wanted a happy ending. Now I prefer the book, but I still think the film is worth watching.',
 en_b1_t2:'Karim has worked as a nurse in a hospital in Leeds for six years. He studied nursing at university because he wanted to help people, and he has never regretted it. However, the job is not easy: he often works at night and at weekends, and he sometimes feels exhausted. Last year he was offered a job in an office with better hours, but he turned it down. “If I worked in an office, I would be bored,” he says. Next September, he is going to start a part-time course to become a specialist in children’s health.',
en_b2_t1:'Should companies adopt a four-day working week? Supporters argue that employees who work fewer hours are more focused and less stressed. In a trial carried out in the UK, most of the participating firms reported that productivity had stayed the same or even improved, and many decided to keep the new schedule. Critics, however, point out that the model does not suit every sector. Hospitals, schools and shops cannot simply close one day a week, so they would need to hire more staff, which would raise costs. Others worry that squeezing five days of work into four could actually increase pressure on workers. In my view, the four-day week is not a magic solution, but it deserves to be tested more widely. Rather than imposing a single rule on everyone, governments should encourage companies to experiment and share their results, so that decisions are based on evidence rather than opinion.',
 en_b2_t2:'When a shocking photo appeared online last spring, showing a shark swimming along a flooded motorway, it was shared more than a million times in two days. It was later revealed that the image had been edited, and that the original photo had been taken years earlier in a completely different place. Cases like this are far from unusual. Researchers have found that false stories tend to spread faster than accurate ones, partly because they are designed to provoke strong emotions such as fear or anger. So what can ordinary users do? Experts recommend pausing before sharing anything that seems surprising. A quick search can often show whether a story has been reported by reliable news organisations. Checking the date and the original source of an image is also useful. Above all, we should remember that a post being popular does not make it true.',
 en_c1_t1:'A growing body of research suggests that access to green spaces has a measurable effect on mental health. In one long-term study, researchers followed more than 10,000 adults over eighteen years and found that individuals who moved to greener areas reported lower levels of anxiety and depression, an improvement that persisted for at least three years after the move. However, the authors were careful to stress that the relationship is correlational rather than causal. People who choose to live near parks may differ in important ways from those who do not: they may have higher incomes, for instance, or more active lifestyles. Although the researchers attempted to control for such variables, they acknowledged that unmeasured factors could still account for part of the effect. Nevertheless, the consistency of the findings across different populations is striking. Several cities have already responded by integrating small parks and tree-lined streets into new housing developments. Whether such initiatives will deliver the expected benefits remains to be seen, but they are, at the very least, unlikely to cause harm. Further experimental research, ideally comparing similar neighbourhoods before and after green spaces are introduced, would help to clarify the mechanisms involved.',
 en_c1_t2:'Few novelists have enjoyed the kind of popularity that Charles Dickens achieved during his lifetime. Most of his novels were not first published as complete books but in monthly or weekly instalments, each costing a fraction of the price of a bound volume. This format allowed readers from a wide range of social classes to follow his stories, and it shaped the way he wrote. Since each episode had to leave readers eager for the next, Dickens often ended chapters at moments of suspense, and he was known to adjust his plots in response to public reaction. It was this close relationship with his audience that made him something of a celebrity. When the final instalment of The Old Curiosity Shop was due to arrive in New York, crowds are said to have gathered at the docks, anxious to learn the fate of its young heroine. Critics have not always been kind to Dickens; some have dismissed his characters as exaggerated and his endings as sentimental. Yet his vivid portrayal of poverty and injustice in Victorian England helped to raise awareness of social problems, and his influence on popular storytelling can hardly be overstated.',
};

